  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/ssao.owl">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology"/>
    <owl:imports rdf:resource="http://protege.stanford.edu/plugins/owl/dc/protege-dc.owl"/>
    <dc:contributor xml:lang="en">Daniel A. O'Neil (NASA, Marshall Spaceflight Center)
daniel.a.oneil@nasa.gov
https://github.com/daoneil/</dc:contributor>
    <dc:contributor xml:lang="en">Robert J. Rovetto</dc:contributor>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3835-7817"/>
    <dc:creator xml:lang="en">Robert John Rovetto

rrovetto@terpalum.umd.edu
http://ontospace.wordpress.com
https://github.com/rrovetto/</dc:creator>
    <dc:description xml:lang="en">The Space Situational Awareness ontology (SSAO) is an ontology for describing the concepts, data, knowledge, objects, events, and interactions of interest in space situational awareness (SSA) for the astronautics and astronomy. 

It formally represent activities and objects in the orbital space environment surrounding a celestial body such as Earth. It also represents the social and technological infrastructure supporting those activities. It expresses our knowledge of these entities, the processes by which we attain that knowledge, together with requisite data and infrastructure. 

The ontology defines categories, relationships, and their instances to capture both generic and specific domain knowledge of the target universe of discourse--the SSA domain (sometimes called 'space domain'). The ontology vocabulary can serve as metadata, semantically annotating data about satellites, as well as the orbital and ground systems supporting them. Defined terms can serve as a standard vocabulary for the community, or contribute to development of (inter)national standards. The project aims for a systematic, accurate and neutral development of ontologies and terminologies that are computable.  

SSA involves past, present and predictive knowledge about the orbital and near space environment, and the processes through which we attain that knowledge and associated data. For example, objects of interest include spacecraft orbiting Earth, datasets about their orbits and motion, and information about the data sources and associated entities. Activities of interest include observation, detection, tracking and identification, as well as the prediction (and simulation) of spacecraft future trajectories. This involves collecting, processing, analyzing and disseminating data from various sources, such as ground-based sensors.  The SSA domain is therefore tentatively construed to include the orbital space and near-space environments, occurrences therein, cataloged data about these entities and associated general knowledge (e.g., scientific laws or principles of astrodynamics).  

The project aims for systematic, accurate and neutral development of these ontologies and terminologies.  

Some purposes and goals of the ontology include: 
(1) To accurately represent objects in orbit, their orbits, and SSA data, processes, actors, and infrastructure.
(2) To support safe spaceflight and space situational awareness.
(3) To provide metadata to link, search, and retrieve data and information systems. 
(4) To provide a semantic layer for databases such as space object catalogs, and information systems, annotating the contained data elements to describe their meaning.
(5) To provide a standard, neutral, widely-applicable and common vocabulary, ontology suite, and knowledge model for the global community.
(6) To develop classifications of space objects such as spacecraft, orbital debris, etc.
(7) To provide a reference model that can be extended by more specialized ontologies.
(8) To be used in academia, industry, and gov't, including university projects, agencies such as NASA, organizations in private sector, mil. projects, and international standards development. 
(9) To explore various applications of semantic technology and artificial intelligence (AI) for astronautics.
(10) To provide an ontological model that can serve as an educational tool for the target domain.

The SSAO is based on [1] "An Ontological Architecture for Orbital Debris Data” by Robert J. Rovetto, published 6 August 2015 in Earth Science Informatics (DOI: 10.1007/s12145-015-0233-3). It, and the ontology of SSA, was discuss in [2] “Preliminaries of a Space Situational Awareness Ontology”, by R.J.Rovetto and T.S. Kelso, in Advances in Astronautical Sciences, and presented at the 26th AAS Space Flight Mechanics meeting. 

The SSAO is a living work, and is in-progress. This particular file was created circa 2013-2016. No claims to completeness are made, and all content is subject to change.

Persons and organizations interested in sponsoring development, contributing to development, using the latest version, or working together, are enouraged to make contact with the author at rrovetto@terpalum.umd.edu or ontologos[at]yahoo[dot]com</dc:description>
