    <ns1:About_the_Creator xml:lang="en">Robert John Rovetto (New York, United States of America) is a formal ontologist, modeler and researcher with a focus area of knowledge modeling for space. Another focus area is abstract, foundational, methodological, and ethical topics in ontology design, development, and use. At the time of this writing, he actively searches PhD study opportunities for this and other projects. He also gives extensive volunteering to both knowledge/semantic modeling groups, standards organizations, spaceflight committees, and emergency response organizations. A lifelong learner, some varied interests are listed at the end of the following.

Contact: 
rrovetto@terpalum.umd.edu, ontologos[at]yahoo[dot]com, robert.rovetto@amu.apus.edu
 
B.A., University of Maryland (UMD). 
M.A., State University of New York.
Coursework in Space Studies, American Military University (APUS)

Member of NASA Datanauts (2017-present):
https://open.nasa.gov/explore/datanauts/2017/spring/

Research Affiliate, Center for Orbital Debris Education &amp; Research, Univ. of Maryland (2016-present).

Member of International Astronautical Federation, AIAA, CCSDS.

GoogleScholar publications webpage:
https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=jTkGEiMAAAAJ&amp;hl=en&amp;oi=sra

Project pages: 
https://github.com/rrovetto/
https://purl.org/space-ontology
https://ontospace.wordpress.com/

ResearchGate page:
www.researchgate.net/profile/Robert_Rovetto

Selected Goals:
1) To continue my education via a studentship.
2) To work for a space organization, such as NASA.
3) To develop ontologies, knowledge models, and terminologies for NASA and similar organizations (in industry, private sector, academia, government, etc.).
4) To find formal collaborations, sponsors, and contributors for this and related ontology projects.

Selected Areas of Interest: knowledge representation and reasoning, formal ontology, astronautics, space situational awareness, orbital debris, AI for astronautics, philosophy, space policy, maritime safety, maritime search &amp; rescue, small-boat operations, foundational ontology topics.</ns1:About_the_Creator>
    <ns1:Desiderata xml:lang="en">The following are desired to sustainably develop this and related works. Please contact the author at rrovetto@terpalum.umd.edu if you'd like to offer any of the following, or otherwise. 

(1) Sponsorship, or other funded opportunities
(2) Formal work collaborations
(3) A PhD study opportunity for the author to sustainable realize the project vision in a stable and mentored educational setting.
(4) Technical expertise and contributions to help with development, and help achieve technical desiderata such as (7-10) below.
(5) Subject-matter experts to help with subject coverage, modeling, and domain content verification.
(6) Application areas, and use-cases, for the ontology(s).
(7) Users of the ontology(s).

Technical desiderata includes:
(7) A graphical-user interface to dynamically search and display terms, definitions, semantic triples, and interactive graph of the ontology. 
(8) A hosting platform.
(9) Datasets to apply the ontology.
(10) Interfacing data and ontology models 
(11) Data sets 
(12) misc.

The project is open to ideas for application and innovation.</ns1:Desiderata>
    <ns1:Development_Status_Note xml:lang="en">The ontology is under development. This particular file represents an incomplete version.

A non-exhaustive list of tasks to be completed include the following (1-7, below). Please contact the author (rrovetto@terpalum.umd.edu) if you are interested in sponsoring, formally collaborating or contributing to development toward realize the project vision.

(1) Identifiers.
All constructs in the ontology (entities, classes, properties, individuals, etc.) are being assigned (i) Identifiers, and (ii) both preferred display labels and alternative or synonymous labels in natural language for human readability. It is anticipated that identifiers will direct to descriptive webpages, or to ontology source files (e.g., OWL files) on GitHub or elsewhere. The IRI's in this particular file may not be active at the time of this application with the NASA Ontology Oriented Orrery (Dr.O'Neil, NASA MSFC).

(2) Definitions &amp; Formalization.
All ontology constructs or terms (e.g. labels) will list associated natural language definitions, and computable definitions. Constraints, axioms and rules are will be asserted, which will support automated inference.

Note: In this file, some constructs may display more than one definition/description, or none. Metadata, and OWL Annotations Properties, will be asserted to specify the type of definition where necessary.

(3) Modeling Alternatives.
Alternative modeling approaches are being explored. For example, Object vs. Data Properties of a similar concept, reflecting modeling attributes and their quantitative values.

(4) Modularization
Portions of the ontology will be modularized into their own reusable ontologies. They can then be imported into other ontology files, such as a master SSAO owl file, or an an external ontology. This reflects a modular ontology architecture according to which the target domain is delineated into sub-domains for more efficient computation, management, and reuse.

(5) Scope &amp; Domain Coverage.
The scope of the SSAO may change. Alternative delineations of the domain may result in a more narrow scope. All modules are intended to be interconnected for a unified and holistic representational framework for the overarching universe of discourse of the space domain.

(6) Knowledge Representation &amp; Reasoning (KRR).
It is anticipated/desired that the ontology will be implemented in more than one KRR language.

(7) Natural Languages
The current language is English, and translation into multiple languages is desired. 

Please contact the author if you're interested in contributing to development, using the ontology(s), or more recent versions.</ns1:Development_Status_Note>
    <ns1:Disclaimer xml:lang="en">The SSAO, in general, is a living work and all content is subject to revision. This including, but is not limited to title, scope, ontology constructs (classes, properties, individuals, metadata), structure, definitions, documentation, etc. 

This is a draft, an incomplete file version, circa 2013-2016. No claims to completeness are made, and it is presented "as is". No warranty.
Please contact the author if interested in more recent versions or interested in supporting development.</ns1:Disclaimer>
    <ns1:Editor xml:lang="en">Robert J. Rovetto</ns1:Editor>
    <ns1:Scope xml:lang="en">The SSAO aims to formally represent entities in, our knowledge of, and data about, the orbital space environment surrounding a celestial body, and occurrences therein. This includes how and from where we acquire that data and information.

It is primarily intended to ontologically represent such things as the orbits of satellites in outer space, the objects themselves (including their parts and features), how they move or behave, the physical and social infrastructure involved in observing and predicting the motion of those objects, the organizations involved, the observation and predictive activities themselves as well as the resultant data, and relevant scientific principles. A special focus is on spacecraft and orbital debris.

The scope of this ontology is that of the SSA or space domain. At the specific instance level, this encompasses the near-Earth environment and the actual phenomena therein, including particular satellites (e.g., the International Space Station, asteroids, etc.), their inter-relationships, how they interact; data about them at a particular time (and over time), and the activities generating that data, and knowledge and situational awareness of the space environment. At a generic level, this involves capturing general knowledge of the orbital and near-space environment (including phenomena therein) for any astronomical body. Combined, this yields what has been called a domain knowledge model, reference model, semantic model, knowledge graph, etc.

The scope more precisely includes the following. 
(a) observation, detection, tracking, identification, and prediction of (artificial and natural) objects, and their past, present and future position and motion in orbit. 
(b) the agents engaging in these activities of achieving actionable SSA.
(c) the infrastructure and resources agents use in SSA-relevant activities and missions, e.g., sensor networks; data processing systems; ground, launch, and space segments.
(d) Data, such as sensor data, and spacecraft telemetry data, collected from various activities and processes.
(e) Data flow processes, e.g., from sensor to spacecraft operator.
(f) Spacecraft and orbital debris, as well as their parts, properties, behaviors, etc.
(g) Events, actions, and processes occurring in orbit, e.g., collisions, spacecraft maneuvers, etc.
(h) predictions and beliefs about current or future space object positions and trajectories
(i) Concepts and scientific principles of astrodynamics, orbital dynamics, etc.
(i) etc.

The scope is subject to change.</ns1:Scope>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Probability_of_Collision">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Orbit_Determination_Concept"/>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3835-7817"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">probability of collision</rdfs:label>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">POC</skos:altLabel>
    <ns1:Alternative_Label xml:lang="en">Collision Probability</ns1:Alternative_Label>
    <ns1:Alternative_Label xml:lang="en">PoC</ns1:Alternative_Label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Gravitational_Orbit">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Orbit"/>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3835-7817"/>
    <dc:creator xml:lang="en">Robert John Rovetto</dc:creator>
    <dc:description xml:lang="en">A curved path desribed by an object under the influence of gravitation. 
The path desribed by one body revolving around another. (See Merriam Webster's Dictiionary). 

Gravitational Orbits are orbits bound by gravitation, and determined by the combination of gravity and the motion of the orbiting object.
Gravitational orbits are gravitationally-bound orbits.
The path of an object orbting a gravitationally-binding mass.
The gravitationally-induced path of an object.
The path traveled by an object bound by gravitation around another object.
The path or motion of an entity (the orbiter) around another entity (the orbited / central entity / central body) due to gravitation. The gravitationally-induced path or motion of an entity aroumd another gravitationally-binding entity.</dc:description>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The types of orbits of interest for the ontology are orbits bound by gravitation, i.e., orbits of objects in astronomical space (the microgravity environment). Therefore, gravitational orbits are orbits that are gravitationally-bound, or the orbits of objects that are gravitationally bound, by some larger mass. The category terms 'Gravitaitonal orbit', 'Astronomical Orbit', or 'Celestial Orbit' are more precise than 'Orbit', but the latter is context-specifically understood to mean the former.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">gravitational orbit</rdfs:label>
    <ns1:Alternative_Label xml:lang="en">Astronomical Orbit</ns1:Alternative_Label>
    <ns1:Alternative_Label xml:lang="en">Celestial Orbit</ns1:Alternative_Label>
    <ns1:Alternative_Label xml:lang="en">Gravitationally-bound Orbit</ns1:Alternative_Label>
    <ns1:Alternative_Label xml:lang="en">Orbit</ns1:Alternative_Label>
    <ns1:Development_Status_Note xml:lang="en">Ongoing development. There are various ways to categorize orbits, e.g., based on a number of subsets of their properties (i.e. orbital parameters). Various definitions and modeling approaches are being developed. This is an ongoing research and development topic by the author since 2009, with papers in-progress.</ns1:Development_Status_Note>
    <ns1:Development_Status_Note xml:lang="en">The label or term 'orbit' may be used as a synonymous label because of its prevalence in the target discipline.</ns1:Development_Status_Note>
    <ns1:Editor xml:lang="en">Robert J. Rovetto</ns1:Editor>
  </rdf:Description>
