  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Definition">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#AnnotationProperty"/>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3835-7817"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A statement expressing the meaning of a word, phrase, symbol, or other semantic or representational construct. 

Examples of constructs in the ontology are those created by the user, and according to the software format or structure. The ontology constructs of the Protege editor include: Class, Object Property, Annotation Property, Data Property, etc.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">There are various types of definition, e.g. stipulative, ostensive, genus-differentia, etc. Sub-annotations of this offer distinct kinds of definition.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Definition</rdfs:label>
    <ns1:Development_Status_Note xml:lang="en">For the ontology, both natural language and computable langauge definitons are desired.</ns1:Development_Status_Note>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Space_Object_Role">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3835-7817"/>
    <dc:description xml:lang="en">A role played by an object location in outer space.</dc:description>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">space object role</rdfs:label>
    <ns1:Development_Status_Note xml:lang="en">Under developent. 
The cateogry of space object does not indicate a persistent ontological category in the metaphysical (philosophy) sense if the category of space object is defined along a locational criteria because that criteria is not invariant. However, this may depend on the intended meaninig of 'space'  since all physical material objects are located in space at all times.</ns1:Development_Status_Note>
  </rdf:Description>
