  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Space_Object">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <dc:creator xml:lang="en">Robert J. Rovetto</dc:creator>
    <dc:description xml:lang="en">an object located in outer space</dc:description>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">space object</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:seeAlso xml:lang="en">https://philpapers.org/archive/ROVSOO.pdf</rdfs:seeAlso>
    <rdfs:seeAlso xml:lang="en">https://philpapers.org/rec/ROVSOO</rdfs:seeAlso>
    <ns1:Development_Status_Note xml:lang="en">Problem: The category of 'space object' is not strictly necessary. Although the lexical term is often used in the spaceflight community, typcially informally, it is not necessarily recommended to denote typical meaning of artificial object in space, because the term is inherently broader and ambiguous. The intended meaning is typically more specific than the term itself, resulting in a mismatch. It does not reflect a stable ontological category. There is nothing in the term that singles out spacecraft as opposed to planets (planets are also space objects).  

If the criteria is location-based, then that meaning reflects an temporary or non-stable criterion: an object in outerspace may fall to Earth, which will no longer make it a space object. 

Solution 1: The concept of roles can be applied. Objects in outer space can be characterized as holding the Space Object Role at one time (e.g., when in Earth orbit), but not at another time (e.g., after landing on Earth's surface).

Solution 2: The concept of Space Object Contact can be used to group those objects in orbit that have been detected. 

Solution 3: Define Space Object to have no ontological (metaphysical) import.

See "Space Object Ontology" by Rovetto, R.J.,  https://philpapers.org/rec/ROVSOO
(a.k.a. "Space Object Ontology and Taxonomy Development")</ns1:Development_Status_Note>
    <ns1:Development_Status_Note xml:lang="en">Under development. There are various ways to categorize objects in outer space, and various permutations are being consider for this portion of the ontology (which will form an ontology file unto itself).</ns1:Development_Status_Note>
    <ns1:IRI_UUID_Shortname>0faf8f8f_7c3b_41a5_ad17_a99c9b913e01</ns1:IRI_UUID_Shortname>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="na5d009298a3a4b9481c2deced7e598abb143">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2003/11/swrl#AtomList"/>
    <rdf:first rdf:nodeID="na5d009298a3a4b9481c2deced7e598abb144"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Astrodynamic_Process">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3835-7817"/>
    <dc:creator xml:lang="en">Robert John Rovetto</dc:creator>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">This is a tentative category for spaceflight activities or processes.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">astrodynamic process</rdfs:label>
    <ns1:Development_Status_Note xml:lang="en">Conceptual development needed.</ns1:Development_Status_Note>
    <ns1:Example xml:lang="en">The change in motion and attitude of a spacecraft when its reaction wheels change rate of rotation.</ns1:Example>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/has_Launch_Vehicle">
