  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Intended_Meaning_of_Ontology_Construct">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#AnnotationProperty"/>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3835-7817"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">This annotation provides, in natural language, the intended meaning or purpose of an ontology construct/element being created, i.e., the symbol structure which takes the form of some set of symbols (whether a human readible phrase or an opaque alphanumeric identifier) and which is associated a human-readible label (distinct from the construct being created).
 
Note: Examples of ontology constructs/elements (or symbol structures) are Classes, Object Properites and Annotation Properties in Protege.

The annotation note should be presented in a way most expressive to the author to maximize comprehensibility and expression of intended meaning/purpose. In this way, this annotaiton expresses descriptive meta-level information combined with object level information, which should match with any definition of the associated label for the given construct.

This reflects a distinction between the description of the construct (the class, relation, or indvidiual), and the lexical definition of the label it is given. 

rdfs:comment and dc:description may be similar, but the idea here is to make the above distinction explicit, i.e., to show the finer detail. 

Consider this: a desription of the term 'dog' (where 'dog' is the value of, say, rdfs:label or skos:prefLabel for the given class construct) may be "a three-lettered word that...", whereas the definition of 'dog' may be "a three-legged mammal that...". 

Therefore, the display/preferred/rendered label (i.e., term) for the construct should have a meaning (definition) that matches it. The two should match--they should express similar, if not the same, intended meaning.

If, however, a label definition is not the same as this, then a definition annotation should provide a more structured definition.

This annotation provides a human-readible statement of the intended meaning or purpose of an ontology element/construct/symbol structure or the human-readible label of that element, i.e., expressing a fundamental understanding of its semantics.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Intended Meaning of the Ontology Construct</rdfs:label>
    <ns1:Development_Status_Note xml:lang="en">Further development needed. 

Disambiguate between Intended Meaninig, Definition, and Clarifying Remark annotations.</ns1:Development_Status_Note>
    <ns1:Example xml:lang="en">This class is intended as a category for all dogs.

NOTE: contrast this will a definition of 'dog' which may be 'a four-legged mammal...'.</ns1:Example>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Orbital_Eccentricity">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Classical_Keplerian_Orbital_Element"/>
    <dc:creator xml:lang="en">Robert J. Rovetto</dc:creator>
    <dc:description xml:lang="en">Eccentricity desribes the shape of an orbit, specifically how much the orbit deviates from a circle. It is represented with  the symbol 'e'.
Closed orbits have an eccentricity greater or equal to 0 but less than 1.
Open orbits have an eccentricity greater or equal to 1.
It is the ratio of half the foci separation (c) to the semi-major axis.
For elliptical orbits it can be calcultated by the apoapsis minus periapsis divided by twice the semimajor axis (or the apoapsis plus the periapsis).</dc:description>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">orbital eccentricity</rdfs:label>
    <ns1:Alternative_Label xml:lang="en">Orbit Eccentricity</ns1:Alternative_Label>
    <ns1:Clarifying_Note xml:lang="en">Defines the shape of the orbit.
Describes the tilt of the orbit</ns1:Clarifying_Note>
  </rdf:Description>
