  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/is_about">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3835-7817"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A generic relation holding between something that can be about something else, such as a data element, dataset, symbol, image, artwork, etc.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">is about</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/About_the_Creator">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#AnnotationProperty"/>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3835-7817"/>
    <dc:description xml:lang="en">An annotation providing information about the ontology creator/author. Example information includes: email addresses, education, affiliations, interests, websites, etc.</dc:description>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">About the Creator</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Polar_Orbit">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Gravitational_Orbit"/>
    <dc:creator xml:lang="en">Robert J. Rovetto</dc:creator>
    <dc:description xml:lang="en">a Low Earth Orbit whose orbital inclination is between approximately 85º and 95º and greater than 104º</dc:description>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">polar orbit</rdfs:label>
    <ns1:Alternative_Label xml:lang="en">Polar Earth Orbit</ns1:Alternative_Label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Geosynchronous_Orbit">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Gravitational_Orbit"/>
    <dc:creator xml:lang="en">Robert J. Rovetto</dc:creator>
    <dc:description xml:lang="en">GEO is an Earth Orbit of an approximate altitude of 35856 km</dc:description>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">geosynchronous Orbit</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Reference_Source">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#AnnotationProperty"/>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3835-7817"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Reference_Source</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Classical_Keplerian_Orbital_Element">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://purl.org/space-ontology/Orbital_Property"/>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://orcid.org/0000-0003-3835-7817"/>
    <dc:description xml:lang="en">In general, the orbital elements are the properties, features, measurable quantities, or parameters required to identify an orbit, and describe the motion of a satellite in orbit. The orbital elements desribe the motion of satellites in an orbit. In general, 6 parameters are needed to uniquely define an orbit and a satellite's position within the orbit. 

The Keplerian (or classical) element describes the size, shape and orientation of an orbit, as well as the location of satellite in the given orbit.

Orbital elements are parameters to describe an orbit.
An orbital element set is a set of quantities specified relative to to a coordinate sysetm and reference frame to describe the orbit of a satelltie. Position and velocity (2 elements) are needed to define an orbit. WIth these elements, a satellites past and future position and velocity may be predicted. 3 parameters each are needed to describe position and velocity in 3-dimensional space. Thus, 6 parameters are needed for an element set.

They describe non-inertial orbital trajectories of the orbiting bodies.</dc:description>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The orbital elements, or specific value ranges thereof, may serve as necessary conditions, or identity conditions, of individual orbits.
An element set has elements as parts.Those elements will have particular values for particular measures of a satellites orbital motion at a time.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">There are many ways to mathematically desccribe an orbit.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Classical Keplerian Orbital Element</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
